"height:1.0306em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose sizing reset-size3 size6 mtight delimcenter\" style=\"top:0.075em;\"><span class=\"delimsizing size2 mtight\"><span class=\"mtight\">]</span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord vbox mtight\"><span class=\"thinbox mtight\"><span class=\"rlap mtight\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8623em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mspace mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.3253em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.3253em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.3253em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text mtight\" style=\"color:green;\"><span class=\"mord mtight\" style=\"color:green;\">gate execution</span></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:1.0474em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3.9267em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen\">{</span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mclose\">}</span><span class=\"mord vbox\"><span class=\"thinbox\"><span class=\"rlap\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8095em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\" style=\"color:blue;\"><span class=\"mord\" style=\"color:blue;\">state space</span></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:5.334em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></p>\n<p>Here <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(-)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> denotes the free monoid/group and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf{B}(-)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbf\">B</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord\">−</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span></span></span></span> denotes forming delooping.</p>","timestamp":1661535851,"subject":"\"programmable computations\" and type families","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100690,"sender_id":492,"sender_full_name":"maxsnew","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/00431e21cdc60799fc8122044f77b917?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/.22programmable.20computations.22.20and.20type.20families/near/100690\">#4</a> Kind of sounds like a <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/transition%20system\">transition system</a> to me, but I’m not sure if that would generalize beyond this example</p>","timestamp":1661543792,"subject":"\"programmable computations\" and type families","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100693,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/.22programmable.20computations.22.20and.20type.20families/near/100693\">#5</a> Thanks, I see. True, every fibration of sets over the delooping of a free monoid defines a “transition system”. </p>\n<p>Maybe I should chase references on transition systems. It would be strange if category theorists hadn’t thought of adjusting the definition of transition systems to the functors that they clearly want to be.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile, as you may have seen, I have found a semi-technical/semi-philosophical text that, for what it’s worth, comes fairly close to saying that computation is path lifting/transport: <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/computation#vanLeeuwenWiedermann17\">here</a>.</p>","timestamp":1661545072,"subject":"\"programmable computations\" and type families","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100699,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/.22programmable.20computations.22.20and.20type.20families/near/100699\">#6</a> Something like a translation from transition systems to path lifting/transport happens around <a href=\"https://www.brics.dk/RS/94/7/BRICS-RS-94-7.pdf#page=12\">pp. 10-12</a> of <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/transition+system#JoyalNielsenWisnkel94\">Joyal, Nielsen &amp; Wisnkel 1994</a> – though it seems somewhat baroque.</p>","timestamp":1661548065,"subject":"\"programmable computations\" and type families","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100706,"sender_id":25,"sender_full_name":"David_Corfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7d52fbe20c8ac05886a296e9ee2159b1?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/.22programmable.20computations.22.20and.20type.20families/near/100706\">#7</a> What comes to mind is <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/type%20refinement\">type refinement</a>. Our page has a  limited view I think. </p>\n<p>From a <a href=\"http://noamz.org/mgs2019/\">course</a> by Noam Zeilberger:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>taking as a guiding principle the naive idea of viewing type refinement systems categorically as “forgetful” functors from a category of typing derivations to a category of terms.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A bifibrational picture as we discussed <a href=\"#narrow/channel/2/topic/Extrinsic.2Fintrinsic.20Curry.2FChurch.20distinctions/near/68605\">here</a>.</p>","timestamp":1661581312,"subject":"\"programmable computations\" and type families","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100708,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/.22programmable.20computations.22.20and.20type.20families/near/100708\">#8</a> Thanks for the pointer – but I have to admit that I am not sure what to make of it.</p>\n<p>What i have in mind is not using any new concepts, just the plain foundations of dependent type theory. All I am saying is: Look, if we say that morphisms/terms-in-context are computations, then we should say that path-lifted morphisms/transported terms are programmed computations (executed programs).</p>\n<p>I think this is clear/obvious – or it will be once you think about it for a minute. What I am really wondering is if anyone has drawn attention to this perspective. In fact, <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/computation#vanLeeuwenWiedermann17\">van Leeuwen &amp; Wiedermann 2017</a> essentially say this, only that they don’t have type-theoretic language available to them (it seems) and the point-set topology which the do invoke falls slightly short of what one would get under type-theoretic semantics in topological spaces – but it’s close enough that one recognizes the idea.</p>\n<p>I have created a tikz graphics that illustrates the point at a glance – temporarily here:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.dropbox.com/s/h3xwdfslvpqzj73/what-is-computation.pdf?dl=0\">pdf</a> (3 pages, overlay)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\,</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span></span></span></span></p>","timestamp":1661592882,"subject":"\"programmable computations\" and type families","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100709,"sender_id":25,"sender_full_name":"David_Corfield","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/7d52fbe20c8ac05886a296e9ee2159b1?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/.22programmable.20computations.22.20and.20type.20families/near/100709\">#9</a> Sounds like the kind of question that would get a good answer on Twitter, if addressed e.g. to Jon Sterling and Andrej Bauer.</p>","timestamp":1661593297,"subject":"\"programmable computations\" and type families","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100712,"sender_id":690,"sender_full_name":"varkor","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/571df014f488b95cc25c203097ee9377?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/.22programmable.20computations.22.20and.20type.20families/near/100712\">#10</a> I’m not quite sure if this is what you’re looking for, but it’s worth taking a look at <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/rewriting\">rewriting</a>, which sounds at least related to your question.</p>","timestamp":1661631089,"subject":"\"programmable computations\" and type families","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100749,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/.22programmable.20computations.22.20and.20type.20families/near/100749\">#11</a> What about if we change the fibration to a (pseudo)functor?  Then this looks to me like the standard situation of categorical semantics, where we have a <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/syntactic%20category\">syntactic category</a> <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Syn}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Syn</span></span></span></span></span> whose morphisms are “names” for computation-functions, and a semantics functor <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Sem} : \\mathrm{Syn} \\to {\\mathrm{Set}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Sem</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8778em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Syn</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Set</span></span></span></span></span></span> that interprets each such name by an actual function that “performs” the described computation.</p>","timestamp":1661750802,"subject":"\"programmable computations\" and type families","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100753,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/.22programmable.20computations.22.20and.20type.20families/near/100753\">#12</a> Yes, that’s the picture!</p>\n<p>I’d like to consider this <em>internal</em> to a type theory since I am after (much as van Leeuwen &amp; Wiedermann <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/computation#ReferencesConceptualizationViaPathLifting\">were</a>) including “continuous programs”, such as the paths in configuration spaces that serve as gates for quantum topological quantum computation; but if there are texts that speak of external semantics functors as encoding computation, I’d be interested.</p>\n<p>For instance, a common question is whether a set of instructions/gates is “universal” for a prescribed notion of computation, and in this picture this means equivalently that the semantics functor is full. That’s the kind of triviality that category/type theorists could be expected to have dwelled on.</p>\n<p>At least that’s what it means classically. For quantum computation and using the internal picture, the usual notion of universality (as in <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/quantum+computation#FreedmanLarsenWang00\">FLW00</a>) means that the semantics functor has <em>dense image</em> on morphisms. This can neatly expressed internal to a suitable modal type theory and is no longer quite as trivial.</p>","timestamp":1661763192,"subject":"\"programmable computations\" and type families","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]}]